    {
      "receiptId": "breadcrumb-apple-cake.html-1",
      "receiptSource": "Original Receipt from \u0027\u003Ci\u003EA Shilling Cookery for The People\u003C/i\u003E\u0027 By Alexis Soyer (\u003Ca href=\u0022references.html#soyer-1845\u0022\u003E\u003Cb\u003ESoyer 1845\u003C/b\u003E\u003C/a\u003E)",
      "receiptText": "\u003Cb\u003E392. Apple Cake.\u003C/b\u003E - Butter a pie-dish near a quarter of an inch thick, throw in a large quantity of bread-crumbs, as much as will stick, when pressed well, on the butter; then have some apples already stewed down and sweetened, of which nearly fill the dish, put one ounce of butter in bits, cover over with bread-crumbs, also half an inch thick, put into hot oven; when done, pass a knife round and turn it out, sugar over, and glaze with a red-hot shovel.\u003Cbr\u003E\n\u003Cbr\u003E\nIf used hot, a little rum put round it and lighted is very nice.\u003Cbr\u003E"
    }
  ],
